<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byzantine Iconoclasm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">730–<a href="/years/787">787 CE</a>; 815–<a href="/years/843">843 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Iconoclast controversy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Byzantine Iconoclasm refers to two periods, 730–787 CE and 815–843 CE, during which Byzantine emperors banned the veneration of religious images and destroyed many icons. The policy provoked bitter religious and political conflict within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and strained relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. It ended with the restoration of icon veneration in 843, still celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byzantine-iconoclasm.jpg" alt="Byzantine Iconoclasm" width="311" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Irenekirken.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
